A service is a named interface. A Layer describes how to build a service and supply its dependencies.
A context holds supplied service implementations.

Build a service once with the dependencies its implementation needs. Return methods that use those captured values.
Callers then require only the service.

## Name the job before the implementation

`Context.Service` names a service and describes its interface.
`Effect.Effect<FileStats, FileStatsError>` declares the method's success and [expected failure](/lore/error-model). Its omitted requirement parameter is `never`.

```ts repo=rat-stack path=packages/core/src/file-inspector.ts at=65e9465f38092e24486392f22c08f45d61230c20 lines=6-15
```

`make` describes service construction as an Effect.
`Layer.effect` runs that construction when the service graph is built.

## Compose work inside the method

Use `Effect.gen` for inline composition. Use `Effect.fn("name")` for reusable functions that need a tracing boundary.
Use `Effect.fnUntraced` when tracing is not useful.
Do not create a function that only returns another `Effect.gen` wrapper.

```ts repo=rat-stack path=packages/core/src/file-inspector.ts at=65e9465f38092e24486392f22c08f45d61230c20 lines=17-35
```

`inspect` retains the filesystem value captured during construction. It keeps read failures typed and returns a domain result.

The service method composes Effects. It does not run a separate runtime that executes the work immediately inside the computation.

## Construction dependencies are not request identity

Methods can retain contextual requirements when the job needs them.
A filesystem implementation usually belongs to construction. A current person may belong to a single request.

Pass request data explicitly, or keep that requirement visible at the request boundary.
Do not capture one request's identity in a service shared by later requests.

[Issue #24](https://github.com/kitlangton/effect-solutions/issues/24) records the risk of treating dependency-free methods as universal law.

## Common wrong approach

Resolving the filesystem inside every `inspect` call leaves `FileSystem` in that method's requirements.
The caller must now know the implementation dependency. Capturing it during construction removes that requirement from the method.

Rebuilding the service inside every call also loses the intended construction lifetime.
Supply its Layer at the [composition root](/lore/layers-make-dependencies-explicit), the startup code that chooses and supplies implementations.

## Effect idiom and house rule

- Services describe behavior. Layers supply implementations and resolve construction requirements.
- In rat-stack, keep `make` and `static layer` beside the service. Core owns service interfaces named for application jobs; adapters own provider code.
- rat-stack also uses capability projections, cartridge packages, and a no-comments rule.

See also: [domain structure](/lore/structure-effect-by-domain), [Layer composition](/lore/layers-make-dependencies-explicit), [the Layer constructor pattern](/lore/layer-constructor-pattern), and [testing through services](/lore/tests-that-earn-their-place).

Read [why dependency injection matters](/lore/dependency-injection-is-the-reason-to-choose-effect).
